+### Speed wave 2: S2-10, saves, loads and town builds with a live screen (2026-11-13)
+
+The speed audit 2's cause C7 for the biggest freezes: a free-play save was one 11.4 s tick, a New story 2.07 s, a new
+island 1.99 s. Measured on this branch with the harness's profiler (`post 1 1`, `profile`): **95% of a save is not the
+flash**. The codec encodes the island twice (the GBA streams: pass 1 counts, pass 2 writes) and for every citizen it
+rebuilds the premade or random base record to compare against (`gene_get` 21%, `gene_set` 20%, `write_town` 14%,
+`res_get_look` 9%, `resident_sanitize` 6%), 5-6 ms a citizen. A load decodes once (6.0 s), a New story builds every
+record once and then runs `sim_init` (2 s). mGBA's flash is near instant; on a cartridge the 4 KB sector erase and
+program add tens of milliseconds a sector.
+
+**What changed (pixel-changing).** The work still runs in one tick: the sim must not move while the island is
+encoded, and the codec is a straight-line encoder, so resuming it across ticks would mean rewriting it. The screen no
+longer sits still. `plat_busy(kind)` and `plat_busy_step(done, total)` (`plat_ext.h`) are called by `save_game`,
+`load_game` and `town_new`, then once per citizen in both save passes and the applying decode, every 32 relationships,
+once per pass of `rel_normalize`'s sort, once per citizen in `sim_init` and once per flash sector. `loadfade.c` (SP5's
+fade) does the rest:
+
+* 50 ms into the job (a quick one shows nothing) the VBlank hook starts the hardware fade, as `plat_loading` does:
+  **a save dims to half, a load or a build fades to black** (BLDY 8 and 16, two steps a vblank).
+* The first `plat_busy_step` after that paints a small card into the Mode 4 page on screen: a pink 112 x 22 box with
+  **"Saving..."** or **"Loading..."** and a ring of eight dots (`vid_rect`, `vid_text`, the resident IWRAM primitives;
+  WIN0 keeps the card out of the fade and the OBJs off it).
+* From then on the **VBlank hook turns the ring one dot every second vblank** with eight halfword stores (no call, no
+  DMA, no overlay code: it runs inside whatever the main thread is doing, a flash sector or a scene's enter) and counts
+  each step as a presented frame in the harness mailbox (`drawn`): 30 pictures a second to the end of the wait.
+* The flip that ends the wait lifts the fade as before (two flips for a save, four for a load) and, when a card was
+  painted, tells the page trackers (`pn` / `hold` / `stage`) that neither page keeps a picture, because the page that
+  had the card is drawn into next. A frame built over several ticks (`hold_building`, I10) or a tile frame on screen
+  gets the fade only. Nothing happens in a run that hashes every frame (`draw_all`).
+
+Why the main thread paints the card and the IRQ only moves it: an IRQ must not run the drawing primitives (the
+interrupted thread may be inside them, and the overlay slot must not be loaded from an IRQ: `iwram_ovl.h`), but a
+dozen raw halfword stores to the page on screen are safe. The first design tried (the saved 2026-11-08 patch) painted
+the card from the IRQ.
+
+| window (`speed2_busy.txt`, libmgba, same commands before and after) | before | after |
+|---|---|---|
+| title -> New story (the build) | 12.2 drawn fps, one 2,076 ms frame | **29.6 fps**, worst picture gap **50 ms** |
+| new island -> hub (the build) | 7.9 fps, one 1,992 ms frame | 18.3 fps, worst gap 117 ms (see below) |
+| save, a Classic island (10 KB) | 3.4 fps, one 10,731 ms frame | **30.0 fps**, worst gap **50 ms** |
+| load, a Classic island | one 6,030 ms command | **29.9 fps**, worst gap **67 ms** |
+| save, the worst case (1,000 custom citizens, 58 KB) | one 11,152 ms command | **30.0 fps**, worst gap **50 ms** |
+| load, the worst case | one 7,355 ms command | **29.9 fps**, worst gap **67 ms** |
+
+`expect-gap 100` holds all but the new island. The table's logic ticks a second stay low by design (3-12): the sim
+holds while the work runs. The new island's remaining 117 ms is not the build: after the card the hub's own first 39
+frames take 67-117 ms (map parts, `fp_island_gfx.c`): S3-14 in the speed audit 3, the same frames as before.
+
+**Same bytes.** `core/tests/test_save_golden.c` fixes the length and CRC-32 of the decoded town section of three
+hand-built islands (no roster, no generator, cut where the first extension chunk starts, so content and chunk changes
+do not move it), taken on `ee686c74` before S2-10. On the GBA the 128 KB flash image `coresave1.txt` leaves (a 58 KB island in slot 1, a
+story island in slot 0) is sha256-identical (`b843ff94...`) between the old ROM and this one, and `make -C gba
+test-coresave` still compares the town CRC across a save and a load in two runs.
+
+**Same pixels everywhere else.** With busy arming switched off, the frame hashes of `entry_parity.txt` and
+`speed_tour.txt` (98 `vhash-seq` windows) are identical to the old ROM's. With it on, only the frames after a new
+island's build differ: the card, the dim, and the hub's held-frame state restarting after the trackers reset.
+
+**Power cut.** `make -C gba test-cutsave` (in `make -C gba test`) stops a save of the worst-case island K video
+frames in (`gba/harness/cutsave.sh`; the harness keeps the flash file in sync as the ROM programs it, so it is what a
+power cut leaves) after a good save of another island, then boots on the file. K = 100 (the first pass writes
+nothing): the old save is intact and loads. K = 350 to 650 (the trailer sector is erased, the sectors are going in):
+the slot reads as empty, never torn. K = 800: the new save verifies and loads with the right town. This is what the
+streamed save always did (the trailer is written last); the GBA keeps one copy per slot, so the old save survives
+only the first half of a save. `core/tests/test_busy.c` cuts the host platform's streamed save at the first, second,
+third, a quarter, half, last-but-one and last write: the previous slot is byte for byte what it was.
+
+**RAM.** EWRAM +16 B resident (`loadfade.c`: the card state, 12 -> 20 B plus padding); IWRAM 0 B (the hook and the card
+are THUMB code in ROM); the main stack's high-water mark is the same 2,780 B through a save and a load; ROM +1,296 B
+of code, +40 B of data.
+
+**Left.** The work is still one tick: a pause or a button press during a save is not seen. The 95% is the base-record
+rebuild per citizen: caching one record per citizen between the two passes (or comparing the stored record to the
+premade one without rebuilding it) would halve a save, and a premade citizen's flags could skip the build entirely.
+That is a separate package.
+
