+**S2-10 (saves, loads and town builds with a live screen).** The PS1's free-play save was one 11.6 s frame: the memory
+card write (PCSX-Redux's OpenBIOS: about 40 ms a 128-byte sector) plus a header peek of a whole 8 KB block after it.
+Title -> New story was frames of 2.4 s and 1.2 s (the town build, seven overlay reads of 314-370 ms), a new island one
+1.9 s frame. The work still runs in one tick; the screen no longer sits still (`plat_ext.h` "S2-10"):
+
+* **The loading screen follows the work.** `plat_busy(kind)` / `plat_busy_step(done, total)` (`gfx.c`) redraw the
+  full-screen loading screen with its bar at done / total, at most every other vblank and only after the work has run
+  three vblanks (a quick job shows nothing). The core calls them per citizen in `save_game`'s encode, `load_game`'s
+  decode, `town_new`, `sim_init`, and per pass of `rel_normalize`. A town build says "Getting everything ready..."
+  without the memory card warning.
+* **Card calls two sectors at a time** (`memcard.c`, `CALL_FRAMES`): a write or read with the loading screen up moves
+  two 128-byte frames per BIOS call and shows a picture after each (the bar used to move once per 8 KB block, once a
+  second). One sector a call was 34% slower overall (the BIOS's per-call cost); two keeps the old total time (the
+  save window 883 vsyncs against 775 with a longer tail of 40 ticks) with pictures 70-100 ms apart. A **peek** (the
+  48-byte save header a menu asks for, the config record) reads the 6-7 frames it needs, not the 64 of a block: the
+  2.3 s the Save/Load screen's refresh took after a save is about 10x less (and every title and menu peek with it).
+  The line under the title says "Writing..." / "Reading..." now ("block N of M" went: 400 B of code).
+* **A spinner over the overlay reads** (`gfx_load_fade`): once the SP5 fade's three darkening passes are done and the
+  drive is still reading (a scene change loads up to three groups; a New story reads seven, 2.4 s), a ring of eight
+  dots turns on the dark picture every other vblank.
+* The harness counts the pictures the game shows outside its loop (`g_busy_pics`: fade passes, spinner steps, loading
+  screens) with the loop's frames (`run.py` `HARNESS_PICS`, `harness.lua` `read_shown`), so `fps` and `gaps` see a live
+  screen. Numbers before this change count loop frames only.
+
+| window (`speed2.sh`, PCSX-Redux) | before | after |
+|---|---|---|
+| title -> New story | 8.5 fps, frames of 2,417 and 1,183 ms | 23.6 fps, worst picture gap **383 ms** |
+| new island -> hub | 16.5 fps, one 1,867 ms frame | 26.2 fps, worst gap **200 ms** |
+| free-play save (16 KB, 3 blocks) | one 11,600 ms frame | 15.4 fps, worst gap **450 ms**, 96 pictures 100 ms apart through the write |
+| load (a Classic island, `speed2_busy`) | not measured | pictures 67 ms apart, worst gap 683 ms |
+
+What is left are single blocking BIOS calls the game cannot slice: the config record's write after a save (380-450 ms),
+the directory scan of an `open` at the start of a load (683 ms), and the scene-enter work after a build (183-383 ms).
+They are one call each in OpenBIOS; on a console the BIOS is faster but not measured. A VSync-callback spinner
+(drawing from the vblank IRQ while the BIOS call blocks) would cover them; it is not built (the card driver is touchy
+about its interrupts, `memcard.c` top).
+
+**Same bytes.** The 128 KB memory card image of a deterministic island save (seed 1, a tick-exact script, `run.py`) is
+sha256-identical (`5b89b9d2...`) between the old and the new disc; `memcard_check.py` reads it (`BASLUS-DOOMO1B`, 2
+blocks, payload 9,718 B, CRC `1fc29482`). The card writes the same bytes in the same order; only the call sizes differ.
+
+**Resident RAM: 320 B less than before.** The banner's and the loading screen's glyph arrays are one (800 B of BSS
+back), the cold busy code is compiled `-Os` by attribute, `rel_normalize` steps once per pass. `_end` 0x801efe60 ->
+0x801efd20, **64 KB below the stack top** with the boot FMV (the margin did not move at the page; the hard 64 KB
+reserve is 416 B higher than the old `_end`).
